1:["$","$Ld",null,{"page":{"id":"library--casual-vs-scripted-vs-careful-speech","kind":"library-article","route":"/roadmap/library/casual-vs-scripted-vs-careful-speech","sourceUrl":"https://refold.la/roadmap/library/casual-vs-scripted-vs-careful-speech","sourceHash":"072721c8f700a816f725edaec744d89a49011e0fa1eec700641ed6b5d6433e25","collectedAt":"2026-09-01T16:52:33.453Z","bodyMode":"authorized-full-body","title":{"en":"Casual vs Scripted vs Careful Speech","zh":"随意口语、脚本化口语与审慎口语"},"description":{"en":"","zh":""},"segments":[{"id":"segment-0001","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Native speakers don't always talk the same way. The register they use varies dramatically depending on the context, and understanding all registers is important for full fluency.","zh":"母语者并不总是以同一种方式说话。他们使用的语域会因语境而发生显著变化；要达到全面流利，理解所有语域都很重要。"}},{"id":"segment-0002","type":"blockquote","content":{"en":"<p>In sociolinguistics, a register is a variety of language used for a particular purpose or particular communicative situation. For example, when speaking officially or in a public setting, an English speaker may be more likely to follow prescriptive norms for formal usage than in a casual setting, for example, by pronouncing words ending in -ing rather than -in, which is common in casual contexts. - <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Register_(sociolinguistics)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">Wikipedia</a></p>","zh":"<p>在社会语言学中，语域是指用于特定目的或特定交际情境的语言变体。例如，在正式发言或公共场合讲话时，英语使用者可能比在随意场合更倾向于遵循正式用法的规范性准则。比如，他们会将以 -ing 结尾的单词读作 -ing，而不是随意语境中常见的 -in。- <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Register_(sociolinguistics)\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">维基百科</a></p>"}},{"id":"segment-0004","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"A few common examples:","zh":"以下是几个常见例子："}},{"id":"careful-speech","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Careful Speech","zh":"审慎口语"}},{"id":"segment-0006","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"This is the clearest, most deliberate way of speaking. <em>Examples: news broadcasts, formal presentations, reading aloud, speaking to a non-native speaker.</em>","zh":"这是最清晰、最有意识的说话方式。<em>例如：新闻播报、正式演讲、朗读、与非母语者交谈。</em>"}},{"id":"segment-0007","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Words are fully pronounced, grammar is standard, and the pace is moderate. This is the easiest register to understand, but also most like to contain embellished lexemes.","zh":"每个词都会完整发音，语法规范，语速适中。这是最容易理解的语域，但也最有可能包含经过修饰的词汇。"}},{"id":"scripted-speech","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Scripted Speech","zh":"脚本化口语"}},{"id":"segment-0009","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Written dialogue that actors perform. <em>Examples: TV shows, movies, audiobooks, radio dramas</em>.","zh":"由演员演绎的书面对话。<em>例如：电视剧、电影、有声书、广播剧</em>。"}},{"id":"segment-0010","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Scripted speech sounds more natural than careful speech, but it's still cleaner and more predictable than real conversation. Words are mostly clear, pacing is designed for the audience, and the vocabulary is chosen for entertainment value.","zh":"脚本化口语听起来比审慎口语更自然，但仍比真实对话更规整、更容易预测。词语大多清晰，节奏会针对受众进行设计，词汇的选择也以娱乐效果为导向。"}},{"id":"casual-speech","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Casual Speech","zh":"随意口语"}},{"id":"segment-0012","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"How native speakers actually talk to each other in real life. <em>Examples: conversations with friends, vlogs, unscripted podcasts, overheard street conversation</em>.","zh":"母语者在现实生活中彼此交谈时真正使用的说话方式。<em>例如：与朋友交谈、视频博客、无脚本播客、偶然听到的街头对话</em>。"}},{"id":"segment-0013","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Casual speech is fast, words blend together, sounds get dropped, grammar bends, slang appears, and people interrupt each other, trail off, and change direction mid-sentence.","zh":"随意口语语速快，词语会连在一起，一些语音会被省略，语法规则也会变得灵活，还会出现俚语。人们会互相打断、话说到一半停住，或在句子中途改变话题方向。"}},{"id":"segment-0014","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Casual speech is the hardest register to understand, but it's also the register you'll encounter most in real life.","zh":"随意口语是最难理解的语域，但也是你在现实生活中最常遇到的语域。"}},{"id":"why-this-matters","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Why This Matters","zh":"为什么这很重要"}},{"id":"segment-0016","type":"paragraph","content":{"en":"Many learners who can understand TV shows are shocked when they can't understand a native speaker talking normally. This is because they've mostly been exposed to scripted speech. Phase 3 deliberately introduces progressively more casual content to bridge this gap, and Phase 3D (Understand Native Conversation) specifically targets casual, conversational speech through crosstalk and unscripted content.","zh":"许多能看懂电视剧的学习者，在发现自己听不懂母语者的日常讲话时会非常震惊。这是因为他们接触的主要是脚本化口语。阶段 3 会有意识地逐步引入更随意的内容，以弥合这一差距；阶段 3D（听懂母语者对话）则会通过交叉对话和无脚本内容，专门训练对随意会话口语的理解能力。"}},{"id":"tips","type":"heading","level":2,"content":{"en":"Tips","zh":"建议"}},{"id":"segment-0018","type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"en":"Don't feel bad if casual speech is hard — it is for us all","zh":"如果随意口语很难，不必感到沮丧——对我们所有人来说都是如此"},{"en":"Vlogs and unscripted YouTube content are good stepping stones between scripted and fully casual","zh":"视频博客和无脚本的 YouTube 内容，是从脚本化口语过渡到完全随意口语的良好跳板"},{"en":"The skills transfer: once you can understand casual speech, scripted and careful speech feel effortless","zh":"这些能力可以迁移：一旦你能听懂随意口语，理解脚本化口语和审慎口语就会变得毫不费力"}]}],"cards":[]},"siteBasePath":"/my_learning_docs"}]
